# The fusions that owe no equality because the overlap was found and could not be sized. One
# row per composed layer and quantity whose elimination is filed as a typed absence, or as an
# identity's output that nothing here computes into the sum.
#
# A different document from an unsearched one
#   It suspends for a different reason. "These hours are double counted and by how much cannot be
#   said" is a composer who looked, found something, and reported the honest width of their
#   ignorance. Reading that as a zero, which `coalesce(e.low, 0)` does, asserts a sum the composer
#   explicitly declined to make.
#
# Grounds at more than one level
#   So the suspension has grounds at more than one level, and a checker that handles only the first
#   is right about the fusions suspended on it and wrong about the rest. They are separate files so
#   each can be run and counted alone, and united in composition/suspension_grounds.sqlc so no
#   caller has to remember them.
#
# The union is a bag
#   One fusion can be suspended on more than one ground and is suspended once, which is why
#   composition/suspended_fusions.sqlc exists as the projection: a caller that counts reads the set,
#   a caller that needs the reason reads the bag.
#
# It carries `quantity` rather than pinning it
#   That is what makes it composable. An elimination is filed per quantity (pm.elimination's key is
#   composition, composed layer, quantity), so filtering `quantity = 'demand'` here and dropping the
#   column makes the union above wear the name "the composed layers that owe no sum at all" while
#   suspending only the demand sum. The first nameplate rule to compose it is then wrong in both
#   directions: suspended because the demand elimination was unsized, and letting a nameplate
#   elimination filed `unmeasured` through into a `coalesce(..., 0)`. The pin in the file name is
#   what makes a caller say which grain it means.
#
# A derived elimination is said to be one
#   A derivation is an output the receiver computes, not a gap, and eliminations/derived.sqlc does
#   find the double counts a shared part implies. Nothing yet wires that figure into the sum, so
#   the sum is still lifted, under the identity's name rather than as an overlap nobody could size.
#
# Any typed absence suspends
#   Not only `unmeasured`. An elimination quantity is a `pm:StatedEliminatedQuantity`, whose three
#   arms are a claim, a named derivation and an absence, so its absent branch is a
#   `pm:ClaimAbsence` carrying `pm:ClaimAbsenceReason`: {unmeasured, notApplicable}, with `none`
#   not in the type. Filtering to `unmeasured` lets the other survive owed_equality and be read as
#   the number zero. tests/composition.rs returns `None` on any typed absence, and the
#   two witnesses must state the same rule.
-- eliminations/filed.sqlc wherever asrt:quantity takes its pm:absent or pm:derivation branch, per quantity.
SELECT e.composition, e.composed_layer, e.quantity,
       CASE WHEN e.derivation IS NOT NULL
            THEN format('the elimination is filed as `%s`, and no computation of it is wired into '
                        'the sum', e.derivation)
            ELSE 'the overlap was found and could not be sized' END AS suspended_because,
       e.reason AS note
FROM (
    :compose(eliminations/filed.sqlc)
) e
WHERE e.absent IS NOT NULL OR e.derivation IS NOT NULL
